<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gia Long</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1762">1762</a>–<a href="/years/1820">1820</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Nguyen Anh, Nguyễn Phúc Ánh, Nguyễn Ánh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gia Long, born Nguyen Phuc Anh, was a survivor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-lords">Nguyen lords</a> who fought the Tay Son for over two decades from bases in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> delta, with help from Siam and the French missionary Pigneau de Behaine. After taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thang-long">Thang Long</a> in 1802 he proclaimed himself emperor, founding the Nguyen dynasty and ruling the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> from Hue, where he built a centralised Confucian state and a great citadel.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gia-long.jpg" alt="Gia Long" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author, restored by Nguyễn Phước Vĩnh Khánh ([1]) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emperor_Gia_Long.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gia Long was born Nguyễn Phúc Ánh on 8 February 1762 in Phú Xuân, now Huế, the third son of Nguyễn Phúc Luân and a grandson of the lord Nguyễn Phúc Khoát. He was a minor member of the family, without support among court powerbrokers, when in 1777 his family was slain in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tay-son-dynasty">Tây Sơn</a> revolt and he, aged 15, went into hiding. His loyalists regained and lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> several times. He befriended the French Catholic bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-pigneau-de-behaine">Pierre Pigneau de Behaine</a>, whom he sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pondicherry">Pondicherry</a> in 1785 to seek French aid. The French authorities there refused to intervene, but Pigneau returned in 1789 with two ships that stayed in Nguyễn service.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>From 1789 Nguyễn Phúc Ánh gained the upper hand. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> region, called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gia-dinh">Gia Định</a>, he re-established a civil service, created a Council of High Officials in 1788 and in 1790 had French officers build a European-style citadel with a perimeter of 4,176 metres. He settled soldiers on fallow land to raise grain, and built up a navy in which European ships served beside traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a>. The death of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tay-son-dynasty">Tây Sơn</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quang-trung">Nguyễn Huệ</a> in 1792 let him march north, and by 1802 he had reunited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, reaching the Qing border.</p><p>As emperor he moved the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> to Huế, built fortresses and a palace there, and restored the Confucian education and civil service examinations that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tay-son-dynasty">Tây Sơn</a> had abolished. He founded the National Academy in 1803 and opened the first Nguyễn examinations in 1807. He asked the Qing Jiaqing Emperor to name the country Nam Việt, and Jiaqing agreed instead to Việt Nam. He abolished large landholdings by princes and nobles and ended the practice of rewarding nobles with a share of village taxes. Under him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> forced Siam out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> in 1813 and kept a garrison in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phnom-penh">Phnom Penh</a>. He tolerated Catholic missionaries in gratitude to the French but refused European trading overtures, among them a British one in 1804.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Gia Long died on 3 February 1820. His line, the Nguyễn dynasty, was the last of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and ruled the unified territory until 1945. His successors, beginning with his son Nguyễn Phúc Đảm, restricted the missionaries he had tolerated. He had many wives and almost 100 concubines, daughters of mandarins, whom he took to secure the loyalty of his circle.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gia Long” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The name Viet Nam was granted by the Qing emperor Jiaqing in 1804, after <b>Gia Long</b> asked for Nam Viet, which recalled the ancient kingdom of Nanyue in southern China.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nguyen-dynasty">Nguyen dynasty · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Siamese forces sent to reinstate <b>Nguyễn Phúc Ánh</b>, who had taken refuge in Bangkok from the Tây Sơn rebels, were ambushed and defeated by the Tây Sơn on the Mekong, in present-day Đồng Tháp province of Vietnam.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-20">20 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>A survivor, <b>Nguyen Anh</b>, fought back from the delta and Siam and in 1802 founded the Nguyen dynasty as emperor Gia Long.</blockquote><a href="/state/nguyen-lords">Nguyen lords</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Nguyen Anh</b>, backed by French volunteers and southern supporters, captured Phu Xuan in 1801 and Thang Long in 1802, founding the Nguyen dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/tay-son-dynasty">Tay Son dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Huế, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. Died in Huế, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=16.46,107.58&amp;year=1799" aria-label="Huế on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7480 1552 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Gia Long"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7930 1822h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7930 1822h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=16.46,107.58&amp;year=1799">What was here in 1799? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
